<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  Entitlements for the hardened runtime, which notarisation requires and which
  the player does not otherwise want.

  One entry, and it is not optional: mpv's scripting runtime is LuaJIT, and
  `lua/zoompan.lua` is what makes zoom and pan land in a single frame (there is
  no way to set several mpv properties atomically from outside — see gotcha 4 in
  CLAUDE.md). The hardened runtime forbids mapping writable-executable memory
  unless the process says it needs to, so without this LuaJIT cannot generate
  code and the script dies.

  Deliberately nothing else. Every entitlement is a restriction lifted, and the
  hardened runtime does not restrict what this application actually does —
  networking, file access and spawning the bundled ffmpeg are all unaffected by
  it (those belong to the App Sandbox, which is not in use here).

  Two that are NOT here and should only be added against a reproduced failure:

    com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory
        The blunter version of allow-jit, for a LuaJIT built without Apple
        Silicon's MAP_JIT support. Try it only if zoom/pan still fails with
        allow-jit alone.

    com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation
        Would let the bundle load libraries signed by somebody else — which is
        exactly what the 35 dylibs in Resources are today, since
        bundle-macos-libs.sh signs them ad-hoc. The fix is to sign them with the
        same Developer ID rather than to stop checking: notarisation rejects an
        ad-hoc-signed Mach-O inside the bundle regardless of this entitlement,
        so disabling validation buys nothing and costs the guarantee.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
  <dict>
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key>
    <true/>
  </dict>
</plist>
